<HelpLayout title="Body tracking" description="Track body weight, measurements and progress photos in Badger, and connect Apple Health or Health Connect.">
  <h1>Body tracking</h1>

  <p>Log body weight and tape measurements alongside your training. Open it from <strong>You &rarr; Body</strong>. Once you've logged a measurement, a small <strong>Body</strong> section appears at the bottom of Progress &rarr; Overview with your latest reading and its trend.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="what-you-track">Choose what you track</h2>
  <p>Only <strong>body weight</strong> is on to begin with. Body fat, waist, chest, arms, thighs and the rest are off until you turn them on.</p>
  <p>Open <strong>What do you track?</strong> from the Body screen's <span aria-hidden="true">&#8942;</span> menu and switch on the ones you want. A cut might be weight plus waist, a bulk weight plus arms and chest, and plenty of people keep it to weight alone.</p>
  <p>Measurements you switch off are hidden. <strong>Switching one off never deletes anything</strong>: turn waist back on next spring and last spring's numbers are still there.</p>
  <p>To track something the list doesn't have, tap <strong>Add a measurement</strong> at the bottom.</p>

  <h2 id="logging">Log a measurement</h2>
  <p>Tap the <strong>+</strong> on a row of the Body screen and type the number. The date starts on today; change it if you're catching up on an earlier reading.</p>
  <p>Tap the row itself to open that measurement's page, with your latest reading, the trend and every entry. Tap an entry to edit it, or its <span aria-hidden="true">&times;</span> to delete it. <strong>Undo</strong> on the message at the bottom brings it back.</p>

  <h2 id="trend">Read the trend</h2>
  <p>Watch the solid accent line: it's a <strong>7-day average</strong>. The faint line with dots is exactly what you logged.</p>
  <p>Body weight moves day to day with water, food and time of day, so one morning's reading can be a kilo off either way. The average smooths that out and shows which way you're really heading.</p>
  <p>Use the <strong>30d</strong>, <strong>90d</strong> and <strong>All</strong> chips to change the time range.</p>
  <p>The small caret next to a reading compares it to the one before. With no <a href="#goals">goal</a> it is plain, since a measurement can be better going up or down. With a goal, a move toward it is green.</p>

  <h2 id="goals">Goals</h2>
  <p>Open a measurement's page, find the <strong>Goal</strong> section and tap <strong>Set a goal</strong>. Type the number you want, which starts at your latest reading, and add a target date if you like. Then save.</p>
  <p>Badger works out whether the goal is above or below your latest reading, so you never pick up or down. The line under the box says how far away it is, for example <em>5.4 kg below your latest reading</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>On the chart</strong> - A dashed line marks the goal. With a target date, a dotted pace line runs from your reading on the day you set the goal to the goal on its date, and the caption gives the pace, for example <em>about 1.4 kg a month</em>. It is a guide only.</li>
    <li><strong>The goal row</strong> - It reads <em>By Mar 1, 2027 &middot; 5.4 kg to go</em>. If the date passes, the goal stays open and the row reads <em>Target date Mar 1 passed</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Reaching it</strong> - Your first reading at or past the goal, logged on or after the day you set it, reaches it. It stays reached even if the number moves back. The row then shows the date and how long it took. Tap <strong>Set a new goal</strong> to replace it.</li>
    <li><strong>On the Body screen</strong> - Under the measurement's name you see <em>5.4 kg to go</em> or <em>Goal reached</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each measurement has one goal. To remove it, use the row's <span aria-hidden="true">&#8942;</span> menu; <strong>Undo</strong> brings it back. Body goals also appear in <strong>You &rarr; Goals</strong>, next to your <a href="/help/goals/">lift goals</a>.</p>
  <p>Goals use your lifting unit, in kg or lbs for weight and cm or in for lengths. Body fat is in %, and a custom measurement keeps its own unit.</p>

  <h2 id="units">Units</h2>
  <p>Measurements follow your lifting unit: kilograms and centimeters, or pounds and inches. Change it and everything switches to the new unit.</p>
  <p>Each entry keeps the unit you typed it in, and switching only changes how it's shown. A weigh-in logged as 81.4 kg stays 81.4 kg, and shows as 179.5 lbs when the app is set to pounds.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="progress-photos">Progress photos</h2>
  <p>Take a front, side and back photo every few weeks and hold any two dates against each other. Photos stay on your phone, in Badger's own storage and not in your phone's photo library.</p>
  <p>To turn it on, open <strong>What do you track?</strong> and switch on <strong>Progress photos</strong>. A <strong>Photos</strong> section appears on the Body screen, with <strong>Check in</strong> and <strong>Compare</strong>.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-check-in">Check in</h3>
  <p>A check-in is one date with up to three photos: front, side and back. Skip any you like. Tap <strong>Check in</strong>, tap a tile to take that photo, and tap <strong>Save check-in</strong>. The date starts on today and you can change it.</p>
  <p>The check-in also shows the body weight and waist you logged that day. Tap <strong>+ Log</strong> beside either to add one. Photos you took are not saved until you tap Save check-in, and leaving first asks whether to discard them.</p>
  <p>Badger shrinks each photo to a JPEG about 1600 pixels on its long edge and removes the location and other hidden details. The original is not kept.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-overlay">Badger's camera and the Overlay</h3>
  <p>The camera shows your last photo of the same pose faintly over the live view, so you can line up your shoulders and hips with it. That faint copy is the <strong>Overlay</strong>. The pill at the top says which photo it is, for example <em>Front &middot; Oct 20 photo overlaid</em>. Tap the <strong>Overlay</strong> chip to turn it off.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guide lines</strong> mark shoulder and hip height.</li>
    <li><strong>Timer</strong> counts down 3 or 10 seconds before the photo, so you can step back.</li>
    <li><strong>Library</strong> picks a photo you already have instead.</li>
    <li>The camera switch flips between the front and back camera.</li>
    <li>After each photo, <strong>Retake</strong> or <strong>Use photo</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On Android, Badger explains why it needs the camera before your phone asks. If you say no to the camera, you can still pick photos from your library.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-tips">Tips for photos you can compare</h3>
  <p>The first time the camera opens, it shows four tips. They are behind the <strong>Tips</strong> chip afterward.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Same spot, same time of day.</li>
    <li>Face a window and avoid light from above.</li>
    <li>Phone at chest height, about 2 m away.</li>
    <li>Use the 10 second timer and stand still.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="photos-past">Add past photos</h3>
  <p>Open the menu on the <strong>Photos</strong> screen (<strong>All check-ins</strong> on the Body screen) and tap <strong>Add past photos</strong>. Pick as many as you like. Each photo takes the date it was taken, or today if it has none, and you can change the date and the pose before you save.</p>
  <p>A check-in holds one photo per pose. If two picks share a date and pose, the list says so, and only one shows in the check-in.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-view">Look back</h3>
  <p>The Photos screen lists every check-in, newest first. Tap a photo to see it full screen with the weight and waist from that time. Pinch or double tap to zoom in, and double tap again to zoom back out. Its menu has <strong>Compare with...</strong>, <strong>Change date or pose</strong> and <strong>Delete photo</strong>.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-compare">Compare and share</h3>
  <p>Tap <strong>Compare</strong> on the Body screen, pick a pose, and Badger opens on your first check-in against your latest. Tap either date to choose another. There are three views:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Side by side</strong> puts the two photos next to each other.</li>
    <li><strong>Slider</strong> lays one over the other; drag to wipe between the two dates.</li>
    <li><strong>Timeline</strong> moves through every check-in of that pose.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Under each date you see the body weight and waist you logged within 3 days of it, and a line with how much each changed.</p>
  <p>On <strong>Side by side</strong>, tap <strong>Share comparison</strong> for one image with both photos, the dates and the readings. Your phone's share sheet opens, and nothing is sent until you pick where it goes. Both photos need to be on your phone to share.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-lock">Lock your photos</h3>
  <p>Turn on <strong>Lock progress photos</strong> from the Photos screen menu and Badger asks for Face ID, Touch ID, your fingerprint or your screen lock before it shows a photo. It locks the Photos screen, the viewer, Compare and the Body thumbnails, which show a plain locked tile. The lock comes back whenever you leave Badger.</p>
  <p>Badger offers the lock once after your first photo. It stays off until you turn it on.</p>
  <p>With the lock on, a photo screen is hidden when you open the app switcher: blurred on iPhone, and left out of the recent apps preview on Android 13 and later. Screenshots still work.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-backup">Back up your photos</h3>
  <p>Photos are only on this phone until you back them up. After your first photo Badger asks where to keep them: <strong>Only on this phone</strong> or <strong>This phone and my iCloud</strong> (<strong>Google Drive</strong> on Android). You can change this on the <a href="/help/settings/#data">Backup</a> screen with <strong>Back up progress photos</strong>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each photo is stored once, in your own iCloud on iPhone or in Badger's private app-data folder on your Google Drive. It is kept apart from your workout backups, so those stay small.</li>
    <li>Android's own backup to your Google account does not carry progress photos.</li>
    <li>Turning <strong>Back up progress photos</strong> off asks whether to keep the copies or delete them.</li>
    <li>Until you back up, the Body screen shows <em>Photos are only on this phone</em>. Tap <strong>Hide</strong> to stop seeing it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Restoring on a new phone brings your photos back from that backup. A photo that has not arrived yet shows <em>Not on this phone</em>.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-export">Export and import</h3>
  <p><strong>Export progress photos</strong> saves a .zip file with your photos as JPEGs and a <code>manifest.json</code> listing each one's date and pose. It is on the <a href="/help/settings/#data">Backup, import and export</a> page. <strong>Import photos from an export</strong> reads one back, from the same page or the Photos screen menu. A photo you already have is skipped.</p>

  <h3 id="photos-delete">Delete photos</h3>
  <p>Delete one photo from its menu, or a whole check-in from its row. Both can be undone from the message at the bottom. <strong>Delete all photos</strong> on the Photos screen menu asks first, then offers <strong>Undo</strong> as well.</p>
  <p>While <strong>Back up progress photos</strong> is on, deleting a photo also removes its copy in your iCloud or Google Drive once the Undo message has gone. <strong>Delete all backups</strong> on the Backup screen deletes your workout backups and keeps your progress photos.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="health">Apple Health and Health Connect</h2>
  <p>Badger can connect to <strong>Apple Health</strong> on iPhone or <strong>Health Connect</strong> on Android in three ways, each off until you turn it on: writing your workouts, importing body weight, and adding heart rate to your sets. Find the switches in <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup, import and export</strong> or the Body screen's <span aria-hidden="true">&#8942;</span> menu.</p>
  <p>Each one asks the health app for its own permission, so turning on heart rate doesn't share your weight history too.</p>

  <h3 id="write-workouts">Writing workouts</h3>
  <p>Every workout you finish is saved to your health store as a strength training session, so your rings, daily summary and other connected apps know you trained.</p>
  <p>Badger writes the start and end time only, with no calorie estimate. Use your watch's figure for calories.</p>
  <p>A back-dated workout isn't written, because with no timer running there's no duration to report.</p>
  <p id="health-offer">You can also turn this on from a workout summary. Once, after a workout, Badger asks <strong>Save workouts to Apple Health?</strong> (<strong>Save workouts to Health Connect?</strong> on Android).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Turn on</strong> asks your phone for permission and saves the workout you just finished.</li>
    <li><strong>Not now</strong> is final: the question doesn't come back. The switch is still in Settings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The question is a <a href="/help/settings/#hints">hint</a>, so turning hints off stops it. It doesn't appear on a summary where you logged an exercise for the first time.</p>

  <h3 id="import-weight">Importing body weight</h3>
  <p>Weigh-ins in your health store (from a smart scale, another app, or typed into Health) appear in your body tracker as ordinary entries. Badger asks for body weight only.</p>
  <p>On Android, Health Connect also asks whether Badger may see data older than 30 days. Allow it and your first import reaches back a year; decline and you get the last 30 days. You can change this anytime in Health Connect.</p>
  <p>Badger checks for new weigh-ins only when you open the Body screen, never in the background. If your health store filled in missing days later, tap <strong>Bring in weigh-ins now</strong> to search the whole past year. The same reading is never imported twice, but a genuine second weigh-in on the same morning is kept.</p>
  <p>Imported weigh-ins are saved in Badger like the ones you type, so they're in your backups: the backup file and, if you use it, the cloud backup to your own Google Drive. See the <a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="heart-rate">Heart rate on your sets</h3>
  <p>Badger saves <strong>one heart rate per set</strong>, taken at the end of the set from the samples your watch has written to your health store. Wear your watch while you train and the numbers turn up with nothing to start or press. Badger reads heart rate; it never writes it back to your health store.</p>
  <p>Two charts show it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>Exertion</strong> chart on a workout summary, under Pace, plots your heart rate set by set across the session, so you can see it drift up as you tire.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Exertion</strong> chart on an exercise's progress page shows your average for that exercise, session by session. If the same weight for the same reps costs you less than it did a month ago, this chart shows it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each set also shows its reading in the set list, in small text with the rest of the set's details.</p>
  <p>The session chart appears once four sets have a reading, and the per-exercise chart once three sessions do.</p>
  <p><strong>Missing numbers are normal.</strong> Watches send samples to the phone in batches, sometimes minutes late, so Badger checks when you finish and again when you open the workout summary. If nothing arrives by then, that set has no heart rate.</p>
  <p>There's no live heart rate while you train. Readings are saved with the set and are part of your backups, like your weights and reps.</p>

  <h3>Turning it off</h3>
  <p>Turn any of the three off whenever you like, or revoke Badger's access in Apple Health or Health Connect. Anything already saved in your body tracker stays until you delete it.</p>

  <h3 id="health-availability">If the switches aren't there</h3>
  <p>On Android versions below 14, Health Connect is a separate app. Install it from the Play Store, then come back to this screen.</p>
</HelpLayout>
